      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Have you struggled with attention and missing details throughout your life, or did this start recently?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Oh, no, it's not a new thing at all. It's been going on my whole life, I suppose. I remember my mother used to keep all my old report cards in this big tin box—used to be a Ritz cracker tin, you know the big round ones? They don't make them like that anymore, now it's all flimsy cardboard that gets crushed in the pantry if you put a can of beans on top of it. \n\nBut she kept that tin in the hall closet, and even way back in elementary school, my teachers were writing the exact same things in those reports. They'd say I was a bright boy, but I was always forgetting my textbooks, losing my homework somewhere between the front door and my desk... and I'd just sit there staring out the window all day unless someone was right there to keep me on track. Usually I was watching the school janitor, Mr. Higgins. He had this old John Deere riding mower. Beautiful machine. Classic green and yellow paint job. My neighbor bought a new John Deere just last summer, but it's not the same engine quality, you know? All the parts are plastic now, they just snap if you look at 'em wrong. I told him he should've looked for a used one, but people just want shiny and new. \n\nBut, uh... yeah, so my teachers definitely noticed I was missing things way back when I was a little kid.",
